    "updated_at": "2018-07-19T10:06:59Z",
    "body": "> My take is that making the HTML step clear is not as much about step 2, as it is about step 4. Maybe we should say something there about how \"compute the text of the box\" takes into account any properties (like visibility) that apply to ::first-letter or ::first-line or similar? What do you think?\r\n\r\nUntil CSS spec changed to mention about `::first-letter` and `visibility` property, innerText should mention `::first-letter` pseudo element at step 2.\r\n> If node's computed value of 'visibility' is not 'visible', then return items.\r\n> Note: Check `visbility` for `::first-letter` and `::first-line` part separately.\r\n\r\n>Separately, we could try to get interoperability on what properties apply to ::first-letter. That is probably best discussed in the CSSWG.\r\nAgree. The CSS spec for ::first-letter and ::first-line should remove \r\n`User agents may apply other properties as well` == this is source of breakage of interoperability,  or at least add black list of CSS properties should not apply `::first-letter` and `::first-line`.\r\n",
